8
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:54,100
Your lifetime tab for wars in countries you can't find on a map: one hundred and thirteen thousand, five hundred and fifty-one

9
00:00:54,100 --> 00:01:00,640
dollars per person over eighty years. That's per person. Every person on Earth.

10
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:09,300
Including you. You personally have been billed one hundred and thirteen thousand, five hundred and fifty-one dollars for

11
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:15,740
explosions on the other side of the planet, and nobody sent you an invoice because they knew you'd cancel.

12
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:24,300
How one percent Less Violence Pays For Everything. An analysis of a decentralized F.D.A.

13
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:29,400
shows pragmatic clinical trials can be forty-four point one times more efficient.

14
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:36,800
Two ways you profit from building one percent fewer bombs: The Captured Money: twenty-seven point two billion dollars a

15
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:45,640
year. Your governments spend two point seven two trillion dollars yearly on things whose sole purpose is making other things

16
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:56,360
stop existing. Redirect one percent: Two point seven two trillion dollars times zero point zero one equals twenty-seven

17
00:01:56,360 --> 00:02:01,360
point two billion dollars. The rounding error on your murder budget.

18
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:06,400
The Bonus Savings: one hundred and fourteen billion dollars a year.

19
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:16,080
Wars cost eleven point four trillion dollars a year. Build one percent fewer bombs, fight one percent fewer wars, save one

20
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:23,700
hundred and fourteen billion dollars. Eleven point four trillion dollars times zero point zero one equals one hundred and

21
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:31,940
fourteen billion dollars. Money no longer spent on unblowing-up hospitals and bridges, removing shrapnel from people you

22
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:39,850
put shrapnel into, housing refugees whose homes you converted into craters, and routing shipping containers around minefields.

23
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:46,140
Your species has an entire industry dedicated to rebuilding things it blew up.

24
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:51,380
You employ people to fill holes that you employed other people to make.

25
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:55,760
On Wishonia, this is used as a children's fable about inefficiency.

26
00:02:56,640 --> 00:03:00,550
The children laugh. Then they ask if it's real. Then they stop laughing.

27
00:03:01,540 --> 00:03:04,560
Where the one hundred and fourteen billion dollars Comes From.

28
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:13,260
Your itemized receipt for violence. Direct costs: seven point six six trillion dollars per year.

29
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:20,480
Military budgets (two point seven two trillion dollars), infrastructure you blow up and then pay to rebuild (one point eight

30
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:27,960
eight trillion dollars), humans you break (two point four five trillion dollars), and trade routes you keep putting mines

31
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:35,650
in (six hundred and sixteen billion dollars). Cut one percent: seventy-six point six billion dollars saved.

32
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:39,920
Indirect costs: three point seven trillion dollars per year.

33
00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:47,360
The part your accountants pretend doesn't exist. Lost economic growth from pointing engineers at missiles instead of medicine

34
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:54,560
(two point seven two trillion dollars). Veteran healthcare, because the humans you send to war come back needing hospitals

35
00:03:54,560 --> 00:04:02,490
(two hundred billion dollars). Refugees, P.T.S.D., environmental damage, lost human capital.

36
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:11,300
Cut one percent: thirty-seven billion dollars saved. Total: eleven point four trillion dollars per year on war.

37
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:15,820
One percent less war saves one hundred and fourteen billion dollars.

38
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:25,040
On your planet, this is called "a good deal." On mine, it's called "so obvious that failing to do it constitutes evidence

39
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:29,620
of a cognitive disability.". Plus cancer gets cured as a bonus.

40
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:37,020
Almost forgot about that part. The benefits cascade beyond medicine in ways that should be obvious but apparently aren't.

41
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:42,940
Addiction is a neurological disorder; cure the brain chemistry and the drug trade collapses.

42
00:04:43,480 --> 00:04:48,680
Most poverty is catastrophic medical costs; cure the diseases and people can work again.

43
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:56,560
Radicalization is often untreated mental illness; you can't bomb someone into being mentally stable (you tried this, it

44
00:04:56,620 --> 00:05:00,080
didn't work, cost eight trillion dollars, created more radicals).

45
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:05,040
Fix the biology and you fix the society. This is not a new insight.

46
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:10,400
It's just one your species keeps forgetting because explosions are louder than pharmacology.

47
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:19,080
The Two Numbers. The Captured Dividend (the money you definitely get): two point seven two trillion dollars in military

48
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:24,600
spending times one percent equals twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year.

49
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:28,300
That's a bank transfer. It happens because the treaty says it happens.

50
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:37,420
No behavior change required. The Societal Dividend (money you save by being slightly less insane): one hundred and fourteen

51
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:43,580
billion dollars per year. Depends on whether one percent fewer bombs means one percent fewer wars.

52
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:53,500
The Elasticity Question. The elasticity parameter (e equals one point zero) asks the only interesting question here: if

53
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:59,220
you cut military spending one percent, do war costs actually drop one percent too?

54
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:04,010
Or do humans, being humans, find a way to keep killing each other at full price?

55
00:06:04,640 --> 00:06:12,740
At e equals zero point two five (the "humans are barely trainable" scenario), only a quarter of cuts reduce violence.

56
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:21,180
At e equals zero point five (the "humans are somewhat rational" scenario, which is historically optimistic), half the cuts

57
00:06:21,180 --> 00:06:29,080
translate. At e equals one point zero (full match, the baseline assumption, probably generous given your track record),

58
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:37,880
every dollar cut is a dollar of war cost saved. At e greater than one point zero, curing cancer together turns out to be

59
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:44,520
better bonding than threatening each other with missiles, which is suspiciously wholesome and therefore the scenario I trust

60
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:53,200
least. It doesn't matter. Even at e equals zero point two five, the Captured Dividend of twenty-seven point two billion

61
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:59,540
dollars is treaty-mandated. That money moves regardless of whether you manage to be less violent.

62
00:07:00,020 --> 00:07:04,770
It's a bank transfer, not a prayer. The Part Where the Math Gets Embarrassing.

63
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:10,100
The guaranteed floor is twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year.

64
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:16,640
Direct budget transfer. No elasticity, no assumptions, no hoping humans behave.

65
00:07:16,740 --> 00:07:24,660
Just math. The best case is one hundred and fourteen billion dollars per year in total societal benefit; the range is wide

66
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:32,280
because, again, your species. Even the pessimistic scenarios stay above roughly fifty billion dollars annually, which means

67
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:38,020
your "worst case" is still the largest increase in medical research funding in human history.

68
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:46,160
For context, the Captured Dividend alone would boost global medical research funding by roughly forty percent over the current

69
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:53,380
sixty-seven point five billion dollar baseline. You've been spending more on camouflage paint than on curing Alzheimer's.

70
00:07:53,980 --> 00:08:01,060
Camouflage paint. To make things invisible. While Alzheimer's makes people invisible and you can't even be bothered to match

71
00:08:01,060 --> 00:08:04,900
the budget. What You're Assuming (and Where It Gets Shaky).

72
00:08:05,940 --> 00:08:09,980
The G.D.P. Multiplier. Spend a dollar on weapons, get sixty cents of economic value.

73
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:14,140
Spend a dollar on healthcare, get four dollars and thirty cents back.

74
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:24,100
Your species chose weapons. Every time. For centuries. The Lost Economic Growth component (two point seven two trillion

75
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:30,640
dollars) already captures this difference. It's not double-counted, just consistently ignored.

76
00:08:31,640 --> 00:08:39,240
The Elasticity Assumption. The treaty requires multilateral cuts, so relative military balance is preserved.

77
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:43,260
If your neighbor also cuts one percent, neither of you is weaker.

78
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:49,720
The Societal Dividend assumes one percent less spending means one percent less war cost.

79
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:57,100
Reality is messier. Some costs lag by years. Some wars are fueled by boredom rather than budgets.

80
00:08:57,560 --> 00:09:05,200
And military spending may occasionally deter a war, though it starts far more than it prevents (a bit like crediting your

81
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:08,100
arsonist neighbor for occasionally calling the fire department).

82
00:09:09,460 --> 00:09:17,280
Here's why the elasticity is probably better than one point zero: military spending doesn't just respond to threats; it

83
00:09:17,340 --> 00:09:25,280
generates them. Robert Pape at the University of Chicago found that ninety-five percent of suicide terrorist attacks from

84
00:09:25,280 --> 00:09:32,140
nineteen eighty to two thousand and three were responses to foreign military occupation, not ideology.

85
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:41,000
Half were completely secular. Your eight trillion dollar War on Terror took terrorist attacks from about one thousand per

86
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:50,460
year to nearly seventeen thousand by twenty fourteen. Bases provoke resistance, resistance justifies bigger budgets, bigger

87
00:09:50,460 --> 00:09:56,560
budgets fund more bases. It's a feedback loop, and feedback loops don't scale linearly.

88
00:09:57,180 --> 00:10:04,460
Cut one percent of the bases, you remove one percent of the provocation, which removes more than one percent of the terrorism,

89
00:10:04,920 --> 00:10:09,580
which removes the justification for more than one percent of next year's budget.

90
00:10:10,300 --> 00:10:18,400
The elasticity isn't one point zero because you're not just reducing spending; you're interrupting the cycle that manufactures

91
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:24,180
the thing you're spending on. It's like discovering that your umbrella was causing the rain.

92
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:30,490
(See the full data on occupation and terrorism.). And you've tested this at scale.

93
00:10:31,180 --> 00:10:39,460
After World War Two, America cut military spending eighty-seven point six percent (from one point four two trillion dollars

94
00:10:39,460 --> 00:10:47,720
to one hundred and seventy-six billion dollars in today's money) and got the greatest economic boom in human history: eight

95
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:53,920
percent G.D.P. growth for a decade, home ownership doubled, the interstate highway system.

96
00:10:54,380 --> 00:10:59,740
The Cold War ended, spending dropped by half, and you accidentally invented the internet.

97
00:11:00,480 --> 00:11:06,580
Every time your species stops buying explosions, prosperity shows up uninvited.

98
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:12,000
You're asking for one percent. You've proven you can handle eighty-seven point six percent.

99
00:11:13,220 --> 00:11:19,620
How Good Are the Sources? I'm being honest about confidence levels, which is more than your defense contractors do.

100
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:27,580
For military spending, the source is the Stockholm International Peace Research Institute, or S.I.P.R.I., which is peer-reviewed;

101
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:35,060
confidence is high. For infrastructure damage, the source is Brown and Watson Costs of War; confidence is medium.

102
00:11:35,540 --> 00:11:39,540
For trade disruption, the source is the World Bank; confidence is medium.

103
00:11:40,220 --> 00:11:44,940
For lost economic growth, the source is S.I.P.R.I. estimates; confidence is medium-low.

104
00:11:45,540 --> 00:11:50,960
For psychological impact, the source is a PubMed meta-analysis; confidence is medium.

105
00:11:51,340 --> 00:11:55,800
For lost human capital, the source is author estimates; confidence is low.

106
00:11:56,620 --> 00:12:04,460
The Safe Bet. For policy decisions, use the Captured Dividend (twenty-seven point two billion dollars) as the reliable number.

107
00:12:05,240 --> 00:12:09,860
Money moved directly to medical research. No assumptions about whether humans will fight less.

108
00:12:10,260 --> 00:12:13,080
Just a bank transfer from the explosion account to the medicine account.

109
00:12:14,080 --> 00:12:17,800
The Societal Dividend is the upside if you manage to behave yourselves.

110
00:12:18,880 --> 00:12:22,660
I'm not holding my breath. (I don't have lungs, but the expression stands.).

111
00:12:23,380 --> 00:12:31,300
The Trajectory, Not the Snapshot. Skeptics ask whether one percent fewer bombs means exactly one percent fewer wars.

112
00:12:31,920 --> 00:12:38,420
This is the wrong question. It's like asking whether the first seed planted produced exactly one tree's worth of shade.

113
00:12:38,980 --> 00:12:46,540
The question is what happens when the seeds keep growing. Your species cut military spending eighty-seven point six percent

114
00:12:46,540 --> 00:12:50,940
after World War Two and got the greatest economic boom in history.

115
00:12:51,900 --> 00:12:58,340
That's the empirical answer. But even that misses the point, because the one percent treaty doesn't stay at one percent.

116
00:12:59,420 --> 00:13:06,660
Incentive Alignment Bonds are tradable securities held by rich and powerful people whose income scales directly with treaty

117
00:13:06,660 --> 00:13:12,260
expansion. At one percent, bondholder payouts are two point seven two billion dollars a year.

118
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:18,300
At two percent, they double. At ten percent, they collect ten times as much.

119
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:25,460
These aren't activists you can ignore at a dinner party. They're investors with Bloomberg terminals, lobbyists, and the

120
00:13:25,460 --> 00:13:33,380
same tools your defense contractors currently use to expand military budgets, except pointed at curing diseases.

121
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:42,280
At just one percent, the I.A.B. political incentive pool (two point seven two billion dollars a year) already outguns your

122
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:48,880
entire defense industry's lobbying budget (one hundred and twenty-seven million dollars a year) by twenty-one point four

123
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:57,520
times. Every expansion multiplies this. You've built a defense lobby in reverse, and it's twenty-one point four times stronger

124
00:13:57,520 --> 00:14:08,380
than the original on day one. Two Futures. Your species currently spends eleven point four trillion dollars a year on war.

125
00:14:08,880 --> 00:14:14,000
Over fifty years at current levels, that's five hundred and sixty-eight trillion dollars.

126
00:14:14,840 --> 00:14:21,580
This is the price tag of the current trajectory: terrorism feedback loops getting worse (Robert Pape showed bases create

127
00:14:21,580 --> 00:14:28,860
the terrorism they're built to stop), an A.I. weapons race that a planet called Moronia already ran to completion, and a

128
00:14:28,860 --> 00:14:35,540
species that spends forty-two trillion on self-destruction when twenty-five trillion would have cured everything.

129
00:14:36,780 --> 00:14:42,020
The other future: bondholders push for treaty expansion because their income depends on it.

130
00:14:42,860 --> 00:14:49,380
Each expansion weakens the terrorism cycle (fewer bases, fewer attacks, less justification for more bases).

131
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:54,040
The A.I. gets trained on curing cancer instead of targeting civilians.

132
00:14:54,880 --> 00:14:59,330
The money compounds in the right direction. Years one to three.

133
00:14:59,520 --> 00:15:04,280
One percent treaty. Research funding is twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year.

134
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:08,160
War costs saved are one hundred and fourteen billion dollars per year.

135
00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:11,680
The treaty passes, and bondholders start lobbying for two percent.

136
00:15:12,340 --> 00:15:18,600
Years four to seven. Two percent treaty. Research funding is fifty-four point four billion dollars per year.

137
00:15:18,680 --> 00:15:22,060
War costs saved are two hundred and twenty-seven billion dollars per year.

138
00:15:22,540 --> 00:15:26,920
Politicians discover voters like living. Years eight to twelve.

139
00:15:27,520 --> 00:15:32,300
Five percent treaty. Research funding is one hundred and thirty-six billion dollars per year.

140
00:15:32,340 --> 00:15:36,100
War costs saved are five hundred and sixty-eight billion dollars per year.

141
00:15:36,780 --> 00:15:40,900
Health lobby rivals defense lobby. Years thirteen to twenty.

142
00:15:41,400 --> 00:15:46,680
Ten percent treaty. Research funding is two hundred and seventy-two billion dollars per year.

143
00:15:46,760 --> 00:15:51,060
War costs saved are one point one four trillion dollars per year.

144
00:15:51,780 --> 00:15:56,100
Defense contractors pivot to health. Years twenty-one to thirty-five.

145
00:15:56,660 --> 00:16:01,640
Twenty-five percent treaty. Research funding is six hundred and eighty billion dollars per year.

146
00:16:01,660 --> 00:16:05,950
War costs saved are two point eight four trillion dollars per year.

147
00:16:06,240 --> 00:16:10,480
Superpowers compete on health outcomes. Years thirty-six to fifty.

148
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:17,060
Fifty percent treaty. Research funding is one thousand three hundred and sixty billion dollars per year.

149
00:16:17,080 --> 00:16:20,980
War costs saved are five point six eight trillion dollars per year.

150
00:16:21,460 --> 00:16:25,320
Historians write confused books about why this took so long.

151
00:16:26,220 --> 00:16:33,600
Over fifty years, the trajectory change is worth one hundred and seventy-five trillion dollars: thirty-three point eight

152
00:16:33,600 --> 00:16:40,920
trillion dollars redirected to curing diseases, plus one hundred and forty-one trillion dollars your species no longer spends

153
00:16:40,920 --> 00:16:48,700
on the industry of blowing things up and rebuilding them. That's at e equals one point zero (one percent spending cut equals

154
00:16:48,700 --> 00:16:56,900
one percent war cost reduction). Given that military spending generates the terrorism it's built to fight, the actual elasticity

155
00:16:56,900 --> 00:17:00,780
is probably better, which means these numbers are conservative.

156
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:08,369
Moronia's politicians were rewarded for buying weapons. Moronia's contractors were rewarded for selling weapons.

157
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:12,040
Moronia's A.I. read the receipts and optimized accordingly.

158
00:17:12,300 --> 00:17:16,290
Every budget vote was an instruction, and the A.I. was a very good student.

159
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:24,319
The Incentive Alignment Bureau, or I.A.B., ratchet flips the incentive structure using the same force that currently points

160
00:17:24,319 --> 00:17:33,060
it toward explosions: money. The difference between Moronia and the civilization that survived wasn't intelligence, resources,

161
00:17:33,160 --> 00:17:40,280
or goodwill. It was whether the richest people on the planet made more money from expanding the treaty or from letting it

162
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:47,800
die. The one percent treaty isn't the peace dividend. It's the down payment on a future so nice that your brain is literally

163
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:50,660
incapable of even imagining it currently.
